hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  James Rachel's in his classic analysis  the challenges of cultural relativism  sets out and isolates five sets of  claims that he takes to be really at the  heart of any formulation of cultural  relativism and he reviews quite a few of  them so I'm coming from anthropologist  so have some coming from our general  Western culture in late modernity and  each of these he sets out so that we can  look at them in turn and see whether  they're actually plausible  so first let's see what each of them is  and then talk a little bit about their  implications what the meaning of them  would be Rachel's own viewpoint on their  interdependence or contradiction and  then some of the rejoinders or  qualifications that he thinks are  important to set out in this piece so  the first is quite simple different  societies have different moral codes the  second the moral code of a society  determines what is right in that society  he actually adds a little bit to that  that is if the moral code of a society  a certain action is right that action is  right at least within that society the  third is there is no objective standard  that can be used to judge one society's  code as better than another's there's no  moral truths that hold for all people at  all time the fourth the moral code of  our own society has no special status it  is but one among many then fifth and  finally it is arrogant for us to judge  other cultures we should always be  tolerant of them so now let's look at  each of these a little bit more closely  different societies have different moral  codes this is supposed to be not so much  a normative statement but a descriptive  statement telling us about how judgments  and evaluations are made about moral  matters within those societies and  rachels gives you several now classic  examples one from Herodotus where the  Greeks are told that you know somebody's  proposing for them to eat their dead and  another group of people are told that  the proposal is that they should burn  their dead and both of them recoil in  horror from what the other group /  supposedly do and this is supposed to  demonstrate to us that there are  different customs different deeply felt  and held beliefs and sentiments about  what's right and wrong and this is quite  true even within the same society we  often have incredible variance among  moral judgments don't we even people  within the same culture disagree about  what is right and wrong the second one  the moral code of a society determines  what is right in that society this is a  somewhat stronger claim if we're  understanding it merely in a descriptive  way then we could say the moral code of  a society - determines for the members  of the society or other people within  that  any trying to take part in it what  counts as right or wrong but this is  going a little bit further it's saying  right and wrong is actually determined  as such by what society that you're in  and it's sort of like you know Pascal's  observation that on this side of the  Pyrenees this is true and on this side  of the Pyrenees this is false different  sides of the Pyrenees of course being  France and Spain the third one there is  no objective standard to judge one  society's code as better than another  with the implication that there are no  moral truths that holds for all people  at all times again a very strong claim  there there's no way to say that one  code or even one portion of a code say  perhaps the parts that have to do with  how to bury or not bury or dispose of or  not dispose of the dead or how long one  should remain in mourning and what one  ought to wear or whether one ought to be  married and what marriage is going to  look like those would all be parts of  codes and then we can talk about the  totality of a cultures code as well this  is saying we cannot judge any of them in  relation to each other doesn't mean that  you can't judge within a particular  society that there would be better and  worse ways of doing things but that  would fall into the number-two category  wouldn't it you're already within that  society at that point the fourth is very  interesting this one is not going to  apply to all societies but to modern  Western industrialized liberal  democratic however whatever  qualifications we want to place on it  our society we should not try to say  that our moral code has any sort of  special status it is merely one among  many different possibilities no better  no  worse than any of them presumably the  moral code that we have today is no  better than the one that we had within  our society say in 1950 or 1850 or 1750  as well so that would be a bit of an  implication of that and then finally the  the probably the strongest claim of all  number five it is arrogant to judge  other cultures we should instead take an  attitude of tolerance towards all of  them this is what the cultural  relativist is telling us as again  members of modern liberal Western  democratic industrial or post-industrial  whatever we are society the cultural  relativist is typically not saying this  to everybody else although perhaps he or  she is thinking it in their mind in  their heart as they approach other  cultures which do not in fact without  your tolerance to the degree that that  we do or at least claim to now it's  quite interesting great shalls' points  out that these five propositions may  seem to go together but they're actually  independent of one another so you could  in fact believe number one without  believing number five or you could in  fact believe number three without  believing number two you could have any  of these together in a combination you  don't actually have to have all five  although he also points out there does  appear to be a bit of a contradiction  between some of them as well and he  gives the example the second says right  and wrong are determined by the norms of  a society the fifth says one should  always be tolerant of other cultures but  what do we do he says in the case of a  society whose norms include intolerance  and the example that he has quite  interestingly has to do with Nazi  Germany invading Poland now think this  through  he says that when the Nazi army invaded  Poland beginning World War two this was  an intolerant act of the  order but what if it conformed to Nazi  ideals you could say well that at the  core of Nazism was this view that races  have to struggle against each other this  is a good thing  who are we to condemn the Nazis who were  even the Polish people who are being  invaded to condemn the Nazi is coming in  rachels has another interesting twist to  this though he says well you know once  they cross that border into polish  society wouldn't they be bound by the  norms of Polish society rather than  their Nazi ideology I mean they are in a  way sort of like resident aliens albeit  within a military organization and so  shouldn't they have given up their  Nazism at the border this is kind of an  ingenious way of talking about this and  you notice that there are some internal  at least tensions if not contradictions  between some of these as well as rachel  is pointing out now towards the end of  his essay Rachel's also says we need to  take each of these and think about the  extent to which they are actually true  or correct or valid what qualifications  would we need to make in order to hold  on to these claims different societies  have different moral codes that is  unmistakeably true there are different  moral codes again I've pointed out even  within the same society subcultures  often vary from each other and  individuals vary from each other  however Rachel's points out this doesn't  mean that the codes are entirely  different from each other as a matter of  fact if you think about certain basic  human functions like say regulating  sexuality or regulating killing every  society every perspective that takes a  moral stand says it's okay to kill in  this case but not in this case and they  vary about what those cases are how to  draw the lines but they do recognize you  can't just kill anybody at any time or  take whatever you want or sleep with  whoever you want so there are  shared norms of some sort number two is  a little bit weaker the moral code of a  society determines what is right in that  society Rachael's points out that when  we assert that we're forgetting a  fundamental distinction the distinction  that every society pretty much  recognizes within its language in its  logic the distinction between believing  something to be the case and actually  being correct about that being the case  because that is true so it's quite  possible that societies could in fact be  mistaken about whether something is the  good thing to do or the right thing to  do or whether it's the wrong thing to do  if not there would be no possibility of  a society actually deciding to change  its norms from within or social conflict  over whether things are right or wrong  the third one no objective standard to  judge one society's code is better than  any other Rachel says well if that's the  case we're in real trouble because that  means that we're not going to be able to  condemn or even judge or probably even  successfully analyze certain cultural  practices that are widely recognized as  wrong and he uses the example of female  circumcision as a prime case we could  also think of genocide as another case  and what Rachel's suggests to us is if  we're going to be able to say that some  things are wrong it is important that  we're not just saying well they're wrong  because they don't fit the American Way  or something like that we're going to  need some what he calls principles that  are not tethered to particular societies  we're going to need something more like  say general moral principles of a  utilitarian oricon she enter some other  kind or a conception of moral rights or  even human rights and then you know  there can be some argument as people  have made that  our Western conceptions but you know the  West is a little bit broader than just a  single particular culture isn't it it's  kind of get it interesting quasi status  is something at least potentially  universal that we can put that aside for  the moment let's look at number four so  if we say that the moral code of our  society has no special status and that  there are many different ways of living  all of them equally valid then does that  actually mean that they're all equally  good it doesn't seem that we have to say  that they're all automatically on  exactly the same level and then finally  with the fifth one it's arrogant to  judge other cultures we should be  tolerant of them and Rachel's wants to  say it simply is not going to work to be  tolerant of everything and he says  there's much truth in this the point is  overstated we are often arrogant when we  criticize other cultures and tolerance  is gender generally a good thing but we  shouldn't tolerate everything and he  says if we want to actually make any  progress as human societies or if we  want to honor the progress that has been  made then we have to say that not  everything can be tolerated and a  general across-the-board  attitude of Tolerance is not actually  morally appropriate so these are the  five claims that Rachel's takes as being  central to most typical portrayal of  cultural relativism and I think he's  actually quite correct about that and  then we can see how each of these has to  be qualified if we want to hold on to  those claims  you
 

